{"name":"Foggy Mist","hex":"#c8d1cc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"foggy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Obscured by mist or fog; unclear; hazy.","Confused, befuddled, or vague."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/foggy"},{"term":"mist","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water or other liquid finely suspended in air.","A layer of fine droplets or particles."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form mist.","To spray fine droplets on, particularly of water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mist"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog"},{"title":"Mist","blocks":[{"text":"Mist is a natural phenomenon caused by small droplets of water aerosols suspended in the cold air, usually by condensation. Physically, it is an example of a dispersion, most commonly seen where water vapor in warm, moist air meets sudden cooling, such as in exhaled air in the winter, or when hot sauna steam is suddenly released outside. Mist occurs naturally as part of weather, typically when humid air comes into contact with surfaces that are much cooler. It can also be created artificially with aerosol spray dispensers if the humidity and temperature conditions are right."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mist"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Minute droplets of water constitute this after-dark radiation fog, with an ambient temperature of −2 °C (28 °F). Their motion trails are captured as streaks.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A close-up view of water droplets forming fog. Those outside the camera lens's depth of field appear as orbs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/500px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/1280px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c